  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nAs of 230 AM Monday morning, a midlevel shortwave trough was \nadvancing northeast through portions of eastern CO. Ahead of the \nshortwave trough, an of low-level WAA extended from southwest KS \nthrough northeast NE. Scattered rain showers were noted within this \naxis of WAA. These widely scattered showers will continue this \nmorning and this afternoon across primarily portions central and\nwestern KS. In addition to the rain showers, dew point\ndepressions near zero combined with light easterly winds were\nresulting in patchy fog across central KS. Visibilities as low\none-half mile are possible through sunrise. Rain chances will \nextend to the I-135 corridor this evening and even towards \nsoutheast KS late tonight into Tuesday morning a secondary weak \nshortwave trough lifts from west TX into the Ozarks. Additional \nrain amounts will generally be 0.25\" or less with the highest \namounts across central KS. \n\nTransitioning into midweek, broad southwesterly midlevel flow will \nremain as another western CONUS trough deepens. Broad low and \nmidlevel WAA may continue to yield isolated to scattered showers \nespecially at night. By weeks-end and into next week, a series of \nweak perturbations may eject across the area, resulting in \nintermittent rain chances.",
